-- #1111 A-2.12 (#1319 unit 2) — THE GUARD AGAINST NARROWING `fieldOwnersRef` BY IMPORT
-- SPELLING.  It pins the property, not the implementation, so it outlives this unit.
--
-- `universeFieldOwners` is unscoped (a record with no import path here still votes in the
-- ambiguity test) and #1070's fourth row is OPEN.  A first cut of unit 2 narrowed the
-- owner set with the same constructor-witness predicates the record half uses, and
-- adversarial review measured an accept->reject regression on exactly this program.  This
-- cell is that program, so the next attempt cannot land the same mistake silently.
--
-- WHY IT DISCRIMINATES, stated so a future reader does not weaken it by accident:
--   * `mkA`'s type is CONCRETE (`RA`), so this is `resolveFieldRecord`'s branch (c), the
--     SECOND consumer of `fieldOwnersRef` — not the undetermined-receiver ambiguity arm
--     that #1070's own repro exercises.  A guard written only against that arm misses this.
--   * the key is `Dup` and the type is `RA`, so `lookupRecordByName "RA"` MISSES and the
--     owners fallback is actually reached.  Rewrite `amod` as a short-form record and this
--     cell silently stops testing anything.
--   * `import amod.{RA, mkA}` has NO `(..)` and no constructor member, so NO constructor
--     is witnessed for the owner key `Dup`.  Importing only `{mkA}` — a value — is equally
--     legitimate and equally witnesses nothing.  That is the measurement that rules out
--     any constructor-spelling predicate here: the question this fallback asks is "could
--     this module be HOLDING a value of that record", which is a reachability question.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ORDERING DEPENDENCY THIS HEADER USED TO RECORD IS GONE — RETIRED, NOT FORGOTTEN.
-- It said: *"this cell is green PARTLY because of an ordering accident … rename `Wrec` to
-- something sorting before `Dup` and this cell REDDENS, for a reason that has nothing to do
-- with the narrowing it guards."*  That was true and MEASURED: on the merge base, renaming
-- `Wrec` → `Arec` gave `Type mismatch: RA vs Arec` at exit 1.
--
-- Q-IDENT (#1383 / #1216) removed it.  `resolveFieldByOwners` now selects the candidate whose
-- `(declaring module, type name)` equals the RECEIVER's before consulting the ladder, so
-- `headL owners` no longer decides anything here and `sortUniqS(["Dup","Wrec"])` is not
-- load-bearing.  The claim is not merely argued: `record_field_owner_ident_unrelated_rename/`
-- IS that renamed program, kept as a live cell, and it prints the same `8` this one does.
--
-- ⚠️ `gen_modules` in `test/diff_compiler_perf_scaling.sh` still requires SHORT-FORM records,
-- and its own reason for that is now the weaker one — a named-field-variant projection would
-- reach the field→owners fallback, which is a cost question rather than the 0-diagnostic
-- question its comment gives.  Read that comment against this note before changing either.
--
-- Expected output, derived BY HAND before any capture and independently of any engine:
--   mkA.dx = 7, mkW.dx = 1, so `8`.
-- Measured on the pre-change binary: `8`, exit 0.  On the first cut of unit 2:
-- `Type mismatch: RA vs Wrec`, exit 1.
import amod.{RA, mkA}
import wmod.{Wrec, mkW}

main : <IO> Unit
main = putStrLn (intToString (mkA.dx + mkW.dx))
